[[local:zz.texts/frege1879-begriffsschriftEnglish.pdf]]

= main =
Frege maintained that like names (and definite descriptions), sentences and
predicates also denoted objects.

- Predicates (and relations) denoted concepts.
  (note that predicates do not have senses!!)
- Sentences denoted truth values (*the True* or *the False*) and expressed
  thoughts(/intensions/senses)
- Names denoted an objects and expressed a sense (a mode of presentation which
  singles out the denoted object).

Just as names have a sense which (together with the state of the world)
determines their reference, so too sentences have a sense (an intension) which
(together with the state of the world) determines which truth value it names.

Just as substituting extensionally equivalent predicates (or descriptions) does
not alter the truth value of [[extensional]] statements; so too substituting one
sentential clause for another naming the same truth value will not change the
truth value of a truth functional statement.

== senses ==
*Freges criterion of synonymy*:
    Two sentences S and S* differ in sense if and only if some rational agent
    who understood both could, on reflection, judge that S is true without
    judging that S* is true. <br>
    (_A Brief Survey of my Logical Doctrines_, 1906)

This is the fundamental principle on which Frege's individuation of senses and
concepts relies.  (Together with substitution arguments - i.e.  that if
replacing one sign with another changes the sense of a containing
sentence those signs must have express different senses [/denote different
concepts]). 

In _Sense and Reference_ Frege explicitly uses this criterion to justify the
claim that sentences express a sense, and denote a truth value:
    It is natural, now, to think of there being connected with a sign (name,
    combination of words, letter), besides that to which the sign refers, which
    may be called the reference of the sign, also what I should like to call the
    sense of the sign, wherein the mode of presentation is contained.
    ... The reference of 'evening star' would be the same as that of 'morning
    star,' but not the sense.
													[[local:zz.texts/frege-philosophicalWritingsEnglish.pdf|_On Sense and Reference_]]
    We now inquire concerning the sense and reference for an entire declarative
    sentence. Such a sentence contains a thought. ...  If we now replace one
    word of the sentence by another having the same reference, but a different
    sense, this can have no bearing upon the reference of the sentence. Yet we
    can see that in such a case the thought changes ... the thought in the
    sentence 'The morning star is a body illuminated by the Sun' differs from
    that in the sentence 'The evening star is a body illuminated by the Sun.'
    Anybody who did not know that the evening star is the morning star might
    hold the one thought to be true, the other false. The thought, accordingly,
    cannot be the reference of the sentence, but must rather be considered as
    the sense.  ...

    We are therefore driven into accepting the truth value of a sentence as
    constituting its reference. By the truth value of a sentence I understand
    the circumstance that it is true or false.  There are no further truth
    values. For brevity I call the one the True, the other the False.  Every
    declarative sentence concerned with the reference of its words is therefore
    to be regarded as a proper name, and its reference, if it has one, is either
    the True or the False.
													[[local:zz.texts/frege-philosophicalWritingsEnglish.pdf|_On Sense and Reference_]]

The criterion of synonymy drives Frege to hold that the same thought can
expressed by sentences with different grammatical subjects:
    The thought itself does not yet determine what is to be regarded as the
    subject. If we say 'the subject of this judgment,' we do not designate
    anything definite unless at the same time we indicate a definite kind of
    analysis; ... we must never forget that different sentences may express the
    same thought.
                                            (fp. 56, [[local:zz.texts/frege-philosophicalWritingsEnglish.pdf|_On Concept and Object_]]) <br>

Senses are objective because they can be shared - you and I use the term
“fire-engine” with the same sense regardless of our personal ideas about fire
engines.  But they are subjective in that they are the “modes of presentation”
by which external objects (and properties) are presented to the mind.  In the
case of names, this seems to amount to taking names to be hidden definite
descriptions.
    A proper name (word, sign, sign combination, expression) _expresses_ its
    sense, _stands for_ or _designates_ its reference.
													[[local:zz.texts/frege-philosophicalWritingsEnglish.pdf|_ibid._]]

== predicates ==
Frege's account runs into difficulties in its treatment of predicates, because
concepts are not objects and yet predicates refer to them.

*concepts are properties*:
    I call the concepts under which an object falls its properties
                                            (fp. 58) [[local:zz.texts/frege-philosophicalWritingsEnglish.pdf|_On Concept and Object_]]

and *predicates refer to concepts*:
    A concept is the reference of a predicate. <br>
                                            (fp. 55, [[local:zz.texts/frege-philosophicalWritingsEnglish.pdf|_On Concept and Object_]]) <br>
    The concept (as I understand the word) is predicative.*
    [footnote: *It is, in fact, the reference of a grammatical predicate. <br>
                                            (fp. 50, [[local:zz.texts/frege-philosophicalWritingsEnglish.pdf|_On Concept and Object_]]) <br>

but *concepts are not objects*:
Concepts are “essentially predicative” and can never be denoted by terms in
subject position:
    The reference of this word [“Venus”] is thus something that can never occur
    as a concept, but only as an object

    A concept is for me that which can be predicate of a singular
    judgement-content, an object that which can be subject of the same. If in
    the proposition “the direction of the axis of the telescope is identical
    with the direction of the Earth’s axis” we take the direction of the axis of
    the telescope as subject, then the predicate is “identical with the
    direction of the Earth’s axis”. This is a concept. But the direction of the
    Earth’s axis is only an element in the predicate; it, since it can also be
    made the subject, is an object. <br>
                            [[local:zz.texts/frege1884-grundlagenDerArithmetik.austinTrans.pdf]]

This leads Frege to make the admittedly “awkward” claim that:
    the concept _horse_ is not a concept
											[[local:zz.texts/frege-philosophicalWritingsEnglish.pdf|_On Concept and Object_]]
on the grounds that whatever “the concept horse” referred to it must be an
object (the sort of thing referred to by terms in subject position) and not a
concept.

Consideration of Frege's success in formal logic can make this awkwardness
understandable. Frege's logic proved to be astonishingly successful in capturing
patterns of inference that were common in everyday speech, and adequate to the
subtleties of mathematics.  This astonishing success was in large part due to a
sharp distinction between predicates and the subject terms to which they were
applied. Frege allowed quantification over variables to in either subject or
predicate position, but the same variable could not appear in both positions.
(see [[Fregean_logic#predicates vs subjects]])

Given the significance of this distinction in his system of logic, it is
understandable that Frege would take this distinction to be fundamental, and read
into it a fundamental distinction in the sorts of objects of which our thoughts
were composed.
    In the enquiry that follows, I have kept to three fundamental principles:
        * Always to separate sharply the psychological from the logical, the
          subjective from the objective;
        * never to ask for the meaning of a word in isolation, but only in the
          context of a proposition;
        * _never to lose sight of the distinction between concept and object_. <br>
										[[local:zz.texts/frege1884-grundlagenDerArithmetik.austinTrans.pdf]]<br>

    I mention an object, when what I intend is a concept. I fully realize that
    in such cases I was relying upon a reader who would be ready to meet me
    halfway—who does not begrudge a pinch of salt.

=== courses of values ===
However, for each predicate there is a corresponding object a “course
of values” of the objects which the predicate maps to the true.
These are the objects that lead to Russell's paradox.
(see [[fregean_logic#courses of values]]!!!)

=== no meaning in isolation ===
DOUBTFUL!!! DOUBLE CHECK
For Frege all predicative terms lacked meaning in isolation
    “every positive integer” does not, as does “the number 20”, by itself yield
    an independent idea but acquires a meaning only from the context of the
    sentence.
                                        (fp. 23) [[local:zz.texts/frege1879-begriffsschriftEnglish.pdf]]
THE ISSUE IS MORE LIKELY THE QUANTIFIER THAN THE PREDICATE HERE.

== definitions ==
*contextual vs denotational* definitions
Frege usually defines a name/term/predicate by specifying the object that it
denotes. Predicates(/relations) name concepts. Sentences name *the True* and
*the False* and express thoughts (/intensions/senses), names refer to objects
and express senses.

However, to specify the meaning of a term is to specify, in principle, how it
forms truths - for a predicate, which objects it is true of. For a term 

However, Frege reaches towards 'contextual definition' at some key points.
According to Frege To completely define a term is to 'theoretically determine'
(not Frege's words - but meaning that in principle there is some way to determine)
which concepts/predicates are true of it. If this includes determining the truth
value of all identity statements containing that term then this collapses the
apparent distinction between denotational definition and
[[contextual definition]].

(Since if we determine what is and is not identical with the object a term
denotes we thereby determine its denotation

most particularly in his definition of natural numbers and in his definition of
courses of values

    Since it is only in the context of a proposition that words have any meaning
    our problem is this: To define the sense of a proposition in which a number
    word appears
                                            §62
											[[local:zz.texts/frege1884-grundlagenDerArithmetik.austinTrans.pdf ]]
											see [[fregean_mathematics]]

== objections ==
==== all necessary truths synonymous ====
(This is a specific application of a general objection to truth conditional semantics.
see [[truth_conditional_semantics#objections#all necessary truths synonymous]]).

An immediate consequence of [[#Freges criterion of synonymy]] is that all
necessary truths are synonymous (this is true of all forms of truth functional
semantics).

But Frege does seem to have a way out here. Even if two statements have the same
truth conditions they may achieve those conditions by combining different
senses. Meanings are structures that fix but are not the same as truth
conditions. This means giving up the criteria above and replacing it with the
following:
{{{
	S and S* differ in sense if some rational agent who understood both could
	judge that S is true without judging that S* is true.
    AND
    Expressions E and E* differ in sense iff there is some sentence S which
    contains E, and substituting E* for E in S produces a statement that a
    rational agent could judge true without judging that S is true
    AND
	S and S* differ in sense iff they have different logical/intensional
	structures or contain expressions with different senses in some position in
	their logical/intensional structure.
}}}
(see [[contextual_definition]])

==== Kripkes modal argument ====
*against names as definite descriptions*
Frege can appeal to the sense of names to explain how there are informative
identity statements. (see [[russellian_semantics#informative identity statements]] )

But treating names as disguised definite descriptions seems to give rise to
further problems:
from: [[kripke1980-namingAndNecessity#lecture 1]]
    1) Necessarily, if Aristotle exists, then Aristotle is Aristotle.
    2) Necessarily, if Aristotle exists, then Aristotle is the greatest
       philosopher of antiquity.

If for each (meaningful) name (e.g. “Aristotle”) there is a definite description
(e.g. “greatest philosopher of antiquity”) expressing the same sense (at least
for one subject or other) then replacing the name with that definite description
should leave the meaning of the sentence unchanged.  However, replacing the last
“Aristotle” in (1) with the matching definite description produces (2) which
seems to have a different truth value. It seems that no matter what definite
description we put in its place the same problem will arise.

However, Frege can maintain that “In the case of genuinely proper names like
'Aristotle' opinions as regards their sense may diverge.” But, “As long as
the nominatum remains the same, these fluctuations in sense are tolerable”. 

Although this only frees Frege from claiming that there is some shared sense of
the name he still appears to have to maintain that there is some such definite
description/sense for each person who can use that name correctly.

However, perhaps there is a sufficiently complex definite description that will
do the job. Consider if Mary claims that Jesus didn't live in Palestine, didn't
live during the 1st century CE, wasn't Jewish, wasn't male, didn't preach about
compassion or service to others and was not crucified. Perhaps she believes
that Jesus is the person who lives at the end of her street.  Perhaps she
maintains that the person who lives at the end of her street is the messiah, the
Son of God and the Son of Man; or that in some other way the man living at the
end of her street possesses some of the properties (the most important ones)
attributed to Jesus in the canonical accounts of him. But maybe she doesn't
believe that anyone even approximately fulfills the description of Jesus given
in the gospels and yet that the person at the end of her street is Jesus.

It seems like a natural reaction is to ask “In what sense are they Jesus?”
It does seem that there may be some truly complex definite description, some
sort of weighted cluster of properties and relations such that if an object
doesn't fulfill them then it is not possible for them to be Jesus (mutatis
mutandis Aristotle). This seems like it could be combined with
[[lewis1972-psychophysicalAndTheoreticalIdentifications]] – It is not possible
for an object that does not come remotely near enough to fulfilling our theory
of Jesus to be Jesus.

If we combine this with the variability of the sense from one speaker to another
it seems like we can get quite a reasonable defence going. The objection is now
that no-one can give this complex definite description on request.

other defences:
	Plantinga, 1978, _The Boethian Compromise_
	Dummett, 1981, _The Interpretation of Frege’s Philosophy_
	Sosa, 2001, _Rigidity in the Scope of Russell’s Theory_
counter:
	Soames, 1998, _The Modal Argument: Wide Scope and Rigidified Descriptions_
			2002, _Beyond Rigidity: The Unfinished Semantic Agenda of Naming and Necessity_
	Caplan, 2005, _Against Widescopism_
a further defence:
	Rothschild, 2007, _Presuppositions and Scope_

==== unknown synonyms ====
from Salmon 1990 “A Millian Heir Rejects the Wages of Sinn”, in _Propositional Attitudes: The Role of Content in Logic, Language, and Mind_
    3) Bob believes that catsup is catsup.
    4) Bob believes that catsup is ketchup.
The objection goes:
(3) is true and (4) is false (by hypothesis Bob falsely believes that these are
two different sauces). So it appears that Frege must deny that “catsup” and
“ketchup” are synonymous. This suggests that terms(mass terms/predicates) are
only synonymous if it is impossible to believe just one of them. It seems that
Frege must multiply senses to an implausible degree. (In brief: if “catsup” and
“ketchup” aren't synonyms then nothing is.)

===== response =====
Bob is using language improperly, or at least idiosyncratically.
In Frege's terms he doesn't “understand both”.  He doesn't have the concept
“catsup” (or even “ketchup”) – which is to say he has no term that expresses the
(identical) senses of those terms. Hence nether (7) nor (8) is true. Bob may
believe that the condiment commonly called “catsup” is commonly called “catsup”
and (erroneously) that the condiment commonly called “catsup” is not commonly
called “ketchup”, but he lacks the concepts usually expressed by the terms
“catsup” and “ketchup”.

Bob is still of course an English speaker, and hence what he is doing is wrong.
But his (norm breaching) error is to have matched up “catsup” with the
wrong concept. He is making a (linguistic) error – but he still doesn't believe
either (3) or (4).
